- historic snapshot option
- docs:
 - README:
  - setup guide
  - example usage
  - comparison to other software
  - contribution guide
 - whitepaper
- warning when using poor password strength (zxcvbn-c?)
- investigate a memory leak in libfuse when using fsstress
- local destination: use inotify (IN_CLOSE_WRITE, IN_MOVED_TO on actions/) to learn about new
  action files instead of listing the directory; keep the full listing every check_interval
  as a fallback (it's needed when the repository is on a network or synced filesystem, which
  inotify doesn't see, and after an IN_Q_OVERFLOW)
- local and ssh destinations: stat actions/ before listing it and skip the listing when its
  mtime hasn't changed; list again when the mtime is very recent (mtime granularity can be 1 s,
  a file added in the same second would be missed)
- retry rejected action files without relying on a full listing (each destination keeps a list
  of the rejected ones), needed once listings get rarer (inotify, mtime check)
- ssh destination: optionally run inotifywait on the server over an ssh exec channel (needs a
  Linux server with inotify-tools and shell access, not sftp-only); fall back to listing
- s3 destination. To decide first:
  - listing only new action files with ListObjectsV2 StartAfter needs action file names that
    sort by time (e.g. a timestamp prefix) -- repository format change, discuss first; needs a
    margin for clock differences between clients and an occasional full listing
  - no directory mtime in S3; S3 event notifications (SNS/SQS/EventBridge) need extra AWS
    setup and aren't available in S3-compatible stores -- probably not worth it
  - HTTP library (libcurl?) and AWS request signing (openssl) -- a new dependency
- PBKDF2-SHA1 with 1 iteration -- crypto-related, discuss first
- aes encryption backend (AES-256-CBC) has no integrity check (MAC); consider authenticated encryption, e.g. AES-GCM
